Design a module that implements the Haar wavelet transform for a set of input signals. The module takes in `n` input signals and produces `m` output signals, where `m` is typically greater than `n`. It processes the input in blocks of 2^n samples, computing averages and differences of adjacent input values to extract hierarchical features. The provided functions calculate the average and difference for pairs of inputs, which are then used to generate transformed output values. The transformation occurs on the rising edge of a clock signal, facilitating timed data processing suitable for applications in signal processing and data compression.
module top_module(
  input clk,                 // Added clock signal as input
  input [n-1:0] in,
  output reg [m-1:0] out     // Changed output to reg type
);

code:
module top_module(
  input clk,                 // Added clock signal as input
  input [n-1:0] in,
  output reg [m-1:0] out     // Changed output to reg type
);parameter n = 4; // number of input signals
parameter m = 8; // number of output signals

// Define a function to compute the average of two input samples
function [n-1:0] average;
  input [n-1:0] a, b;
  begin
    average = (a + b) >> 1;
  end
endfunction

// Define a function to compute the difference of two input samples
function [n-1:0] difference;
  input [n-1:0] a, b;
  begin
    difference = (a - b) >> 1;
  end
endfunction

// Compute the Haar wavelet transform for each block of input signals
// The input signals should be processed in blocks of 2^n samples
// The output signals should be the result of the Haar wavelet transform
always @ (posedge clk) begin
  integer i; // Declare i as an integer
  for (i = 0; i < n; i = i + 2) begin // Use n as the loop limit
    out[i] = average(in[i], in[i+1]);
    out[i+1] = difference(in[i], in[i+1]);
  end
end

endmodule

